From 4e0902c413e926954c288a3670faaca816029fac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pandemonium M9 Agent <agent@pandemonium.dev>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 11:28:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] feat(ai): the wave machine presses on a cadence and
 closes the kill (M9)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The M9 tuning pass over the scripted Alpha opponent, measured on the
headless AI-vs-AI runner across 32 seeds: every match now resolves
(winner declared by stage 10) between ~9100 and ~23300 ticks — inside
the fifteen-minute budget the M8 exit names, where the M8 flagship
stalled unresolved at any budget.

Three changes, each addressing a stall the diagnosis exposed:
- While a wave is alive, its pressure timer re-issues the march at the
  cadence (WAVE_TIMER_TICKS). A wave whose orders drained — a failed
  march at the crossroads choke, a completed march over a cleared
  target, a defense pull that brought the army home — used to park
  forever: the 'army >= MIN_WAVE' liveness check read the parked camp
  as an active press. Now a live wave never parks.
- The march focuses the enemy command center when it is in sight
  (the elimination target, plan §9.7) through the fog-filtered view,
  falling back to the enemy start.
- Waves of ten (from six) with an army cap of sixteen (from twelve):
  waves of six traded forever in low-count attrition cycles that never
  accumulated the sustained damage to level a defended base; ten with
  the round-robin Guardian mix closes in one successful press.

View-level unit tests re-pinned to the new constants, plus a new test
pinning the re-march itself (a quiet wave re-issues on the cadence and
aims at the sighted CC). The 7200-tick flagship golden moved (controller
decisions are canonical hashed state) and is re-pinned with the written
reason; tick 0 is unchanged.
