The M9 feedback cues, all event-driven (FD-9: the draw loop drains
the step's events outward; nothing polls the simulation):

- Hit flashes: every AttackHit marks its victim for 18 frames; the
  wgpu renderer pushes a flashing entity's team color toward hot
  white (selection brightening still wins), through a new
  presentation-only Frame.flashes field the null renderer ignores.
- Health bars: damaged entities draw a backing rect and a
  green/amber/red fill above their projected head — the
  hp_fraction_milli field RenderEntity has carried since M6 finally
  drawn. Projected through the same camera the box select uses.
- Command pings: a fading crosshair at the clicked ground position
  the frame the command is issued — the immediate
  intent-to-visible-response cue (A8's tick-bounded motion is the
  sim-side half, already pinned by the M4 suite).

feedback.rs is pure geometry over plain data, so five unit tests pin
it without a GPU: the NDC -> pixel mapping, the bar color bands and
fill fraction, flash collection/expiry, ping expiry. The headless
smoke now drives the windowed path's exact hosting seam
(MatchHost::with_controllers with the AI opponent) and feeds the
same sink + feedback state the draw loop uses, printing the wiring
counters as evidence (events fed, cues mapped, attacks flashed).
Restart (R) resets the feedback state with the match.

The frozen-chase-order fix in the first M9 commit was this pass's
responsiveness half: an army that answered a dead target forever
was the loudest feel bug in the game.
